.Dd September 8, 2026
.Dt BOSD 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bosd
.Nd on-screen display engine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl hv
.Op Fl n Ar instance
.Bro Fl d | Fl C Brc
.Nm
.Op Fl Dhov
.Op Fl n Ar instance
.Op Fl A Ar opacity
.Op Fl a Ar text
.Op Fl B Ar seconds
.Op Fl b Ar badge
.Op Fl F Ar color
.Op Fl G Ar color
.Op Fl g Ar percent
.Op Fl O Ar opacity
.Op Fl P Ar percent
.Op Fl p Ar text
.Op Fl s Ar scale
.Op Fl x Ar offset
.Op Fl y Ar offset
.Bro Ar icon | Fl c Ar countdown | Fl T Ar text Brc
.Op Ar hold_seconds
.Nm
.Op Fl Dhv
.Op Fl n Ar instance
.Op Fl B Ar seconds
.Op Fl F Ar color
.Op Fl G Ar color
.Op Fl g Ar percent
.Op Fl P Ar percent
.Op Fl x Ar offset
.Op Fl y Ar offset
.Fl t Ar text
.Op Ar hold_seconds
.Nm
.Op Fl Dhv
.Op Fl n Ar instance
.Op Fl B Ar seconds
.Op Fl G Ar color
.Fl g Ar percent
.Op Fl P Ar percent
.Op Fl x Ar offset
.Op Fl y Ar offset
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is an on-screen display engine: it flashes transient,
click-through feedback over the desktop, the kind a desktop
environment shows for media keys and hotkeys.
Feedback takes five forms: a PNG glyph scaled to the panel and
outlined for legibility, giant countdown digits
.Pq Fl c ,
large outlined text
.Pq Fl T ,
small caption text
.Pq Fl t ,
and a gauge bar
.Pq Fl g .
Glyph, digits, and large text composite directly over the desktop,
centered on the monitor marked primary
.Pq see Xr xrandr 1 ;
when no primary is set, on the laptop's built-in panel
.Pq eDP/LVDS/DSI ;
and failing both, on the X screen as a whole.
Small text centers near the bottom of the same panel, its top
fixed 205 pixels up.
The gauge bar runs on an independent timer, so a gauge and a
glyph can be up at once: a new gauge replaces the gauge, new
artwork replaces the artwork, and neither clears the other.
A warm daemon keeps both on screen and repaints in place, so
rapid key repeats never flash.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-n instance"
.It Fl A Ar opacity
Multiply the artwork fill's alpha by
.Ar opacity
.Pq 0.0 to 1.0 .
On an
.Ar icon ,
without
.Fl A
the file's baked alpha is left alone
.Pq e.g. the example BSD glyph at 80% ;
with
.Fl A
the outline halo is still drawn from the file's native coverage and
stays at its
.Fl O
opacity while only the glyph is multiplied
.Pq an 80% pixel with Fl A Li 0.5 becomes 40%;
.Fl A Li 0
leaves an outline with an invisible glyph .
On
.Fl T ,
.Fl A
fades the white fill
.Pq default full without Fl A .
Requires an
.Ar icon
or
.Fl T ;
it does not apply to
.Fl c
or
.Fl t .
.It Fl a Ar text
Caption below the artwork: a smaller outlined line under the
glyph, digits, or text, e.g.
.Dq airplane mode off
under the airplane glyph.
Escapes decode as with
.Fl T ;
literal whitespace is permitted.
At most 63 characters before decoding.
.It Fl B Ar seconds
Hold the gauge bar for
.Ar seconds
.Pq default 3.0, clamped to 0.01-30 ,
independent of the hold on the glyph, digits, or text.
A value of \-1 holds the bar until it is replaced or cleared.
On a warm daemon the first
.Fl B
of a bar session is latched like
.Fl P :
later paints keep that hold
.Pq so omitting
.Fl B
cannot turn an indefinite hold back into the default 3 s
and each paint still refreshes a finite deadline from now.
Requires
.Fl g .
.It Fl b Ar badge
Draw
.Ar badge
as a superscript at the glyph's upper right
.Pq about a quarter of the glyph height, black-outlined ;
fill color is
.Fl F
.Pq default white .
Used for index digits and short labels
.Pq e.g. a headphone number, Dq All .
At most 31 characters before decoding; literal whitespace is not
permitted.
Escapes decode as with
.Fl T ,
so
.Ql a\ex20b
badges as
.Dq a b .
.It Fl C
Clear the channel's display, artwork and gauge alike.
An in-progress countdown or an indefinite
.Pq \-1
hold included, returning immediately.
Without a running daemon there is nothing to clear.
.It Fl c Ar countdown
Count down from
.Ar countdown
to 1 in huge outlined digits on a full-panel overlay, one second
per digit
.Pq Ar hold_seconds overrides ,
then exit.
Replaces
.Ar icon
and is dispatched to the channel daemon like an icon, replacing
whatever it displays
.Pq and vice versa ;
without a daemon the
invoking process draws it and returns when done.
Honors
.Fl b , F , o , s , x ,
and
.Fl y :
the badge sits at the digits' upper right and
.Fl s
scales the digits;
.Fl F
colors the badge
.Pq not the digits .
.It Fl D
Render directly in the invoking process, ignoring any running
channel daemon, and exit when the hold elapses.
Useful for a quick look without disturbing what the daemon
displays.
.It Fl d
Run as the display daemon for
.Ar instance ,
listening on a per-user datagram socket.
.It Fl F Ar color
Fill text and badges with
.Ar color ,
a name or #rrggbb.
On
.Fl t
the default is green; on
.Fl T
and
.Fl b
the default is white.
Applies to
.Fl t
and
.Fl T
text, and to
.Fl b
when the badge adorns an
.Ar icon ,
.Fl c ,
or
.Fl T .
.It Fl G Ar color
Fill the gauge bar's ticks with
.Ar color ,
a name or #rrggbb
.Pq default green #2AC12A .
.Nm
assigns the bar no meaning
.Pq the caller encodes states such as muted through the color .
Requires
.Fl g .
.It Fl g Ar percent
Draw the gauge bar: 56 ticks bottom-centered on the panel, 64
pixels up, filled ticks tall and the rest short
.Po
the classic xosd bar look, drawn by
.Nm
itself with no library dependency
.Pc .
Above 100% the fill stays full and the percentage is written just
past the bar's right edge in the same color.
With
.Fl P
on a warm daemon, short ticks at or above the latched previous
percent use a 50% dimmer shade of the fill; short ticks between
the current fill and that watermark stay full color
.Pq the return zone .
Tall ticks are never dimmed.
Alone it shows only the bar; alongside an
.Ar icon ,
.Fl c ,
.Fl T ,
or
.Fl t
the two go up together, each expiring on its own hold:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -b 2 -g 200 -G orange audio-headphones
.Ed
.Pp
Honors
.Fl x
and
.Fl y .
.Ar percent
is 0 to 9999.
.It Fl P Ar percent
Previous gauge percent for the watermark under
.Fl g
.Pq 0 to 9999, or \-1 to omit .
When the bar is not yet up, the daemon latches the first
.Fl P
and ignores later ones until the bar hides or is cleared.
Without a daemon,
.Fl P
still dims that one-shot paint.
Requires
.Fl g .
.It Fl h
Print usage and exit.
.It Fl n Ar instance
Select the OSD channel
.Po
default
.Dq Li default
.Pc .
Each channel has its own overlay and socket.
.It Fl o
Skip the black outline normally drawn behind the glyph for
legibility on any wallpaper.
Suited to full-color artwork that carries its own contrast.
Conflicts with
.Fl O .
.It Fl O Ar opacity
Multiply the black outline halo's alpha by
.Ar opacity
.Pq 0.0 to 1.0, default 1.0 .
The halo is still drawn from the file's native coverage; only its
opacity changes
.Pq Fl O Li 0
hides the outline while leaving the glyph.
Requires an
.Ar icon
or
.Fl T
with outline enabled; conflicts with
.Fl o .
.It Fl p Ar text
Caption above the artwork, otherwise as
.Fl a ,
e.g.
.Dq Shutdown in
over a countdown, with
.Fl b Li s
marking seconds.
.It Fl s Ar scale
Multiply the panel-derived render size by
.Ar scale
.Pq 0.1 to 8.0, default 1.0 .
The glyph is normally sized from the panel height, independent of
the PNG's native resolution;
.Fl s Li 2.0
renders it twice that size.
.It Fl T Ar text
Draw
.Ar text
in the countdown's giant outlined style, centered on the panel and
held like an icon
.Pq e.g. the checkmark confirming a screenshot .
Replaces
.Ar icon
and is dispatched like one.
Escapes decode before rendering:
.Ql \ex Ns Ar NN
a raw byte,
.Ql \eu Ns Ar NNNN
and
.Ql \eU Ns Ar NNNNNNNN
a Unicode codepoint
.Pq UTF-8 encoded ,
and
.Ql \e\e
a backslash
.Po
so
.Fl T Ql \eu2713
draws the checkmark
.Pc .
Literal whitespace is not permitted; embed it as
.Ql \ex20 .
Honors
.Fl A , b , F , o , O , s , x ,
and
.Fl y .
.It Fl t Ar text
Draw
.Ar text
as small caption lines: 24-pixel fixed type, black-outlined,
centered near the panel bottom with the first line's top fixed
205 pixels up, filled with the
.Fl F
color
.Pq default green ;
the look and place of a screenshot's filename caption.
Lines split on newline
.Pq escape Ql \ex0a ,
center individually, and grow downward.
Replaces
.Ar icon
and is dispatched like one; the gauge bar is independent.
Escapes decode as with
.Fl T ;
literal whitespace is not permitted
.Pq embed it as Ql \ex20 .
Honors
.Fl x
and
.Fl y .
.It Fl v
Print version information and exit.
.It Fl x Ar offset
Shift the artwork horizontally by
.Ar offset
pixels from panel center: positive right, negative left.
Placement semantics are those of
.Fl y .
.It Fl y Ar offset
Shift the artwork vertically by
.Ar offset
pixels from panel center: positive down, negative up
.Pq X11 coordinate sense .
Consumers sharing a screen with fixed chrome
.Pq e.g. a taskbar
can
recenter within the remaining space, such as
.Fl y Li -20
above a 40-pixel bottom bar.
Offset placement is exact; artwork carried past a panel edge is
clipped at the edge rather than shown on a neighboring output,
so a large offset slides the artwork in from beyond it.
Without offsets, placement is clamped to remain fully visible.
.El
.Pp
Without
.Fl d ,
.Nm
hands the request to the running daemon for the channel; if no
daemon is alive, it paints the glyph, bar, or text itself and
exits when
.Ar hold_seconds
elapse
.Pq default 2.0 for glyph or text, default 3.0 for bar; clamped to 0.01-30 .
A
.Ar hold_seconds
of \-1 holds the display until it is replaced or cleared with
.Fl C ;
without a daemon, until interrupted
.Pq e.g. Ctrl-C .
With
.Fl c ,
.Ar hold_seconds
times each digit, so \-1 is rejected: the digits must advance.
.Pp
An
.Ar icon
containing a slash is used as a path.
A bare name is searched in each directory of the colon-separated
.Ev BOSD_PATH ,
then in the compiled share directory.
A
.Pa .png
suffix is appended when missing.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "BOSD_PATH"
.It Ev BOSD_PATH
Colon-separated icon search path, tried before the compiled default.
.It Ev DISPLAY
X11 display to draw on.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/tmp/bosd.instance.uid.sock" -compact
.It Pa /tmp/bosd. Ns Ar instance . Ns Ar uid Ns Pa .sock
request socket
.It Pa /tmp/bosd. Ns Ar instance . Ns Ar uid Ns Pa .pid
daemon pid
.It Pa @PREFIX@/share/bosd
default icon directory
.It Pa @PREFIX@/share/examples/bosd
glyph authoring example
.Pq Pa bsd.py
and the shared rasterizer
.Pq Pa glyph.py
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Warm the airplane-mode channel at session start:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n airplane -d &
.Ed
.Pp
Flash the on glyph for 1.5 seconds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n airplane airplane-on 1.5
.Ed
.Pp
Show the second headphone output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n audio -b 2 audio-headphones 1.5
.Ed
.Pp
Screenshot flow
.Pq count down from 3, capture, confirm with a checkmark :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n shot -c 3
scrot screenshot.png
bosd -n shot -T '\eu2713' 1
.Ed
.Pp
Announce the saved file
.Pq two green caption lines at the panel bottom :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n shot -t 'Screenshot\ex0ascreenshot-2026.09.08.png' 2
.Ed
.Pp
Clear whatever the channel is showing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n shot -C
.Ed
.Pp
Shutdown warning: caption over a 10-second countdown, badge
marking seconds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n power -p 'Shutdown in' -b s -c 10
.Ed
.Pp
Caption a glyph:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n airplane -a 'airplane mode off' airplane-off
.Ed
.Pp
Build the example glyph
.Pq the letters BSD in FreeBSD red at 80% opacity on a transparent ground
and flash it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
python3 @PREFIX@/share/examples/bosd/bsd.py bsd.png
bosd bsd.png
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bosd 3 ,
.Xr xrandr 1
